<h2>When does the production timeline begin?</h2>
<p>Production does not begin as soon as the initial files are submitted. We first confirm what the model needs to communicate and define its scale, content, level of detail, materials, finishes, features, and delivery requirements.</p>
<p>The production schedule is established once the required information is available and the scope is approved.</p>

<h2>What happens during the project?</h2>
<h3>Scope and file review</h3>
<p>We confirm the model's purpose, audience, dimensions, detail, materials, schedule, display requirements, and delivery constraints.</p>
<p>Usable CAD, BIM, or 3D files are then reviewed and prepared for fabrication. If production-ready geometry does not exist, separately scoped Digital Model Development may be required.</p>
<h3>Samples and approvals</h3>
<p>When needed, samples or mockups are produced to confirm materials, colors, finishes, lighting, connections, or important details.</p>
<p>Timely feedback and approval help keep the project on schedule.</p>
<h3>Fabrication and assembly</h3>
<p>Components are produced using the appropriate combination of laser cutting, CNC machining, 3D printing, painting, assembly, and skilled handcraft.</p>
<p>The time required depends on the model's size, component count, materials, level of detail, and functional features.</p>
<h3>Finishing and quality control</h3>
<p>The model is finished, assembled, inspected, and tested. Lighting, removable sections, moving components, and other functional elements require additional review.</p>
<p>Progress and quality-control information are shared through the Carve Studio Portal.</p>
<h3>Packaging and delivery</h3>
<p>Packaging is designed around the model's size, fragility, destination, and handling requirements. Large models may require <a href="/blog/architectural-model-packaging-shipping-installation">sectional construction, specialized crating, coordinated transportation, and on-site assembly</a>.</p>
<p>Delivery and installation time should be included in the overall project schedule.</p>

<h2>What can extend an architectural-model schedule?</h2>
<h3>Incomplete or changing design information</h3>
<p>Missing elevations, unfinished landscaping or changes to the building after fabrication begins may require components to be redesigned and remade.</p>
<h3>CAD and BIM preparation</h3>
<p>Revit and Rhino files frequently contain internal elements, furniture, MEP systems and geometry that are unnecessary at the selected physical scale. Preparing and simplifying these files can add time before fabrication begins.</p>
<h3>Model size and sectional construction</h3>
<p>Large models may need to be divided into transportable sections. Connections, alignment, protective cases and installation requirements must be coordinated before production.</p>
<h3>Architectural and landscape detail</h3>
<p>Façade patterns, balconies, interior spaces, custom trees, vehicles, furniture and small architectural elements require additional fabrication and hand finishing.</p>
<h3>Lighting and interactive features</h3>
<p>Integrated LEDs, illuminated roads, removable floors, moving components and interactive controls require electrical coordination, testing and access for future maintenance.</p>
<h3>Samples and finish approvals</h3>
<p>Custom colors, façade materials and landscape finishes may require samples or mockups. Production continues after the client approves the selected direction.</p>
<h3>Client review and revisions</h3>
<p>The speed and clarity of client feedback directly affect the total elapsed schedule. The 14-21 day internal production target pauses when Carve Model is waiting for required approvals or revised information.</p>
<h3>Packaging, freight and installation</h3>
<p>Large or fragile models may require sectional construction, custom crates, freight delivery, inside delivery or on-site assembly. These requirements should be established before fabrication.</p>

<h2>How can you keep the project on schedule?</h2>
<p>Define the model's purpose and audience early. Provide the most current design information available, identify the final deadline and delivery location, and confirm important decisions before fabrication begins.</p>
<p>If the model is required for a planning hearing, investor presentation, sales-gallery opening, exhibition, or another fixed event, include that date in the initial enquiry. We can then work backward to identify the required approval, production, and delivery milestones.</p>

<h3>How clients can avoid production delays</h3>
<p>Before production begins, provide:</p>
<ul>
<li>Confirmed model boundary</li>
<li>Preferred scale or maximum dimensions</li>
<li>Revit, Rhino, SketchUp, DWG, PDF or other available files</li>
<li>Current plans, elevations and site information</li>
<li>Required buildings and landscape elements</li>
<li>Color and material references</li>
<li>Lighting and removable-component requirements</li>
<li>Display-base and protective-cover requirements</li>
<li>Final delivery address</li>
<li>Required presentation or installation date</li>
<li>One designated person responsible for consolidated approvals</li>
</ul>

